What was adapted: the idea of numbered, strongest-first patterns of machine-written
prose, the "weak alone" rule for patterns that need company, and the structure of the
pattern families. Pattern wording and all examples in this library are new and written
for narrative fiction. Humanizer's patterns come from Wikipedia's "Signs of AI writing"
(https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Signs_of_AI_writing).

The craft frameworks in the plot, character, magic-worldbuilding, scene-craft, and
foundations skills are restatements, in original words, of ideas taught in Brandon
Sanderson's 2025 lecture series on writing science fiction and fantasy. No lecture text
is included. This project is not affiliated with or endorsed by Brandon Sanderson.

The Story project folder layout (a kb/ folder for settled story facts and a work/ folder
for working documents) is compatible with the layout used by haowjy/creative-writing-skills
(https://github.com/haowjy/creative-writing-skills, Apache License 2.0) so that projects
built with that library can be adopted without being reorganized. No text or code from
that library is included.

Several ideas were adapted, as ideas, from wgwtest/novel-writing
(https://github.com/wgwtest/novel-writing, MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 wgwtest):
separating action that changes a scene from decorative and procedural motion in dialogue,
a deterministic text-hygiene check, protecting style-bearing material during revision,
first-entry anchors for important characters, learning from the writer's own edits, short
chapter cards with tiered reading, causal bridges and reveal boundaries in planning, and
limits on what a character can know in a scene. All wording, examples, and code in this
library are new. No text or code from that project is included.

Further ideas were adapted, as ideas, from three other MIT-licensed projects:
- xbraindance/Creative-writing-skill (https://github.com/xbraindance/Creative-writing-skill):
  offering options that differ in kind, after the Verbalized Sampling paper it cites
  (Zhang et al., arXiv 2510.01171).
- zenstory-ai/oh-story-claudecode (https://github.com/zenstory-ai/oh-story-claudecode):
  deterministic checks on a draft for a cut-off ending, repetition loops, and length against a
  target; foreshadowing tracked with IDs and payoff status; a size budget for skills.
- danjdewhurst/story-skills (https://github.com/danjdewhurst/story-skills): the question of what
  a continuity check can find reliably, and the idea of a promise ledger the tools can read.
All wording, examples, and code in this library are new. No text or code from those projects is
included.
